The '''Jawi–Sago high-speed railway''' (Burawi: ''Wadada Tirtarka ee Jawi-Sago'') is a [[high-speed rail]] line connecting [[Jawi]], the capital of [[Kijura]], with [[Sago]] in neighbouring [[Burawa]]. The line, which opened in 2018, was constructed as an upgrade of the existing 19th-century railroad corridor, with tracks rebuilt to high-speed standards and grade-separated throughout its length. At 140 kilometres, of which 22 kilometres lie within Kijura and 118 kilometres within Burawa, it is the primary rail link between Kijura and the Burawi rail network.

The line is operated as a joint venture between [[BuRail]], the Burawi state railway, and [[Kijura National Railways]], the national railway operator of Kijura. It serves an estimated 7,500 passengers daily as of 2024. Cross-border passengers clear customs at [[Sago Central railway station]], where both Kijuran and Burawi customs facilities are co-located.

== History ==

=== Background ===

The original railroad connecting Sago to [[Hargiesa]] (then Galwa's capital, now in [[Fayeha]]) via Kijura was constructed in the 1880s and 1890s. The line traversed Kijura, providing transit revenue and a limited economic stimulus, though the region's sparse population resulted in modest utilisation. Following [[Fayeha]]'s independence in 1948, the line was rerouted to connect to [[Saranka]], Fayeha's new capital, rather than Hargiesa.

By the early 21st century, the existing infrastructure had deteriorated, and the winding alignment through the coastal hills limited speeds to approximately 80–100 km/h. Meanwhile, growing economic ties between Kijura and Burawa, particularly after the Socialist Party gained dominance in Kijuran politics in the 1980s, increased demand for faster cross-border transport.

=== Construction ===

Planning for a high-speed rail link began in the early 2010s, with a formal agreement signed between the governments of Kijura and Burawa in 2014. Construction commenced in 2015 and was completed in 2018. The project involved upgrading the existing alignment to high-speed standards, including:

* Rebuilding tracks to support speeds of up to 250 km/h
* Grade-separating all road crossings along the route
* Installing overhead electrification (25 kV AC)
* Constructing new station facilities at Jawi and Sago

The total cost of the project was shared between the two governments, with Burawa providing technical expertise and rolling stock through BuRail.

=== Opening ===

The line opened to passenger service in 2018, reducing travel time between Jawi and Sago from approximately 90 minutes on the legacy line to approximately 35–40 minutes. The opening ceremony was attended by Lord [[Maxamed Kaahin]] and senior Burawi officials.

== Route ==

The Jawi–Sago high-speed railway runs for 140 kilometres from [[Jawi]] on the Aethiopian Ocean coast to [[Sago]] in southwestern Burawa. The line follows the general alignment of the original 19th-century railroad, though numerous curves have been eased to accommodate higher speeds.

The route traverses the coastal hills of Kijura before crossing into Burawa near the border settlement of [[Dhagax]]. From there, it descends into the Burawi lowlands to reach Sago.

