=== Border crossing and customs ===

The international border between Kijura and Burawa is located approximately 22 kilometres from Jawi. All cross-border passengers are required to clear customs and immigration controls. To facilitate efficient cross-border travel, customs facilities for both countries are co-located at [[Sago Central railway station]], the terminus of the line. Passengers clear Kijuran exit controls and Burawi entry controls at the station before proceeding to their destination.

This arrangement, established as part of the 2014 bilateral agreement, eliminates the need for on-board customs inspections and allows passengers to clear formalities at their convenience before boarding or after arrival.

== Operations ==

=== Service frequency ===

Services operate at approximately hourly intervals throughout the day, with increased frequency during peak periods. The first train departs Jawi at approximately 06:00, with the last service at approximately 22:00.

=== Rolling stock ===

The line is operated using high-speed electric multiple units provided by BuRail. The rolling stock is capable of speeds up to 250 km/h, though average operating speeds are somewhat lower due to acceleration and deceleration time.

=== Journey times ===

The typical journey time between Jawi and Sago is 35–40 minutes, a significant reduction from the approximately 90 minutes required on the legacy line prior to upgrading.

== Ridership ==

The line carries an estimated 7,500 passengers daily as of 2024. Ridership has grown steadily since opening, driven by increasing cross-border economic activity and tourism. The line is particularly popular with business travellers and commuters working in Sago but residing in Jawi.

== Impact ==

The opening of the high-speed railway has substantially reduced travel times between Kijura and Burawa, strengthening economic ties between the two countries. The line has also facilitated increased tourism to Kijura, as visitors from Burawa can now reach Jawi in under an hour from Sago.

The project has been cited as an example of successful cross-border infrastructure cooperation in Southwestern Kaftia.

